@base = http://localhost:5119
@alice = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
@bob = 22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222

### The state of the grid: units (at least 2), routing, replication, pump, database
GET {{base}}/grid

### A request: the X-Unit header tells which unit answered (with affinity, always the same one for Alice)
GET {{base}}/accounts/{{alice}}

###
POST {{base}}/accounts/{{alice}}/withdrawal
Content-Type: application/json

{ "amount": 10 }

### The same data in each replica and in the database (run right after a withdrawal: replication takes 100 ms)
GET {{base}}/grid/comparison/{{alice}}

### Transfer: debit by Alice's unit, credit requested from Bob's unit
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 25 }

### ─── Collisions ───

### With affinity: 200 simultaneous withdrawals of 1 €, a single unit handles them, nothing is lost
POST {{base}}/demo/concurrent-withdrawals
Content-Type: application/json

{ "account": "{{alice}}", "count": 200, "amount": 1 }

### Switch to round robin…
POST {{base}}/grid/routing
Content-Type: application/json

{ "mode": "RoundRobin" }

### … and start again: several units write Alice at the same time, writes get lost (lostUpdates),
### the final balance no longer matches the accepted withdrawals
POST {{base}}/demo/concurrent-withdrawals
Content-Type: application/json

{ "account": "{{alice}}", "count": 200, "amount": 1 }

### Back to affinity
POST {{base}}/grid/routing
Content-Type: application/json

{ "mode": "Affinity" }

### ─── Elasticity ───

### 600 simultaneous account openings: the manager adds units (up to 6), then removes them once calm
POST {{base}}/demo/load
Content-Type: application/json

{ "count": 600 }

### A few seconds later: back to 2 units; the log tells the story
GET {{base}}/grid/log

### Add, stop cleanly, bring down a unit (replace u1 with an identifier from GET /grid)
POST {{base}}/grid/units

###
DELETE {{base}}/grid/units/u1

###
POST {{base}}/grid/units/u2/failure

### ─── The database out of the request path ───

### The database goes down…
POST {{base}}/grid/database/failure

### … the requests keep going (200); the writes pile up in the pump (pumpPending)
POST {{base}}/accounts/{{bob}}/withdrawal
Content-Type: application/json

{ "amount": 1 }

###
GET {{base}}/grid

### All the units go down: no grid in memory any more, and no database to reload it → 503
POST {{base}}/grid/total-failure

###
GET {{base}}/accounts/{{bob}}

### The database comes back: the next request waits for the pump to write everything, then reloads the grid (cold start)
POST {{base}}/grid/database/restore

###
GET {{base}}/accounts/{{bob}}
